---
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const byOrder = (a: { data: { order: number } }, b: { data: { order: number } }) => a.data.order - b.data.order;
const research = (await getCollection('projects', (p) => p.data.featured && !p.data.draft)).sort(byOrder);
const allWriting = (await getCollection('writing', (w) => !w.data.draft)).sort(byOrder);
// skip posts already linked from a Research entry
const researchUrls = new Set(research.flatMap((p) => p.data.links.map((l) => l.url)));
const notInResearch = (w: (typeof allWriting)[number]) => !researchUrls.has(w.data.url);
const writing = allWriting.filter((w) => !w.data.personal && notInResearch(w)).slice(0, 3);
const personal = allWriting.filter((w) => w.data.personal && notInResearch(w));
const meta = (w: (typeof allWriting)[number]) => [w.data.venue, w.data.role].filter(Boolean).join(' · ');

// a project links to its blog post if it has one, otherwise to its entry on /research
const href = (p: (typeof research)[number]) =>
  p.data.links.find((l) => !l.url.startsWith('TODO'))?.url ?? `/research/#${p.id}`;
---

<Base title="Ioana Frîncu (Frincu): research on AI evaluation and safety" jsonLd>
  <header class="hero">
    <div class="portrait-wrap" id="portrait">
      <div class="flip">
        <img class="portrait face front" src="/photo.jpg" width="168" height="168" alt="Portrait of Ioana Frîncu in a black bucket hat" />
        <img class="portrait face back" src="/meme.jpg" width="168" height="168" alt="The evil plotting raccoon meme: a raccoon rubbing its paws together" aria-hidden="true" loading="lazy" />
      </div>
      <button class="raccoon-btn" id="meme-toggle" type="button" aria-pressed="false" aria-label="Reveal my fav meme template" data-tip="reveal my fav meme template">
        <img src="/raccoon.svg" width="24" height="20" alt="" />
      </button>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h1 class="name">Ioana Frîncu</h1>
      <p class="links" id="contact">
        <a href="https://github.com/flifenstein">GitHub</a>
        <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/frincui">LinkedIn</a>
        <a href="https://scholar.google.com/citations?user=Y2m2NaoAAAAJ&hl=en">Scholar</a>
        <a href="https://flifenstein.com">Blog</a>
        <a href="/cv/">CV</a>
        <span>f_ioana [at] tuta [dot] com</span>
      </p>
      <p>
        I want to know when we can trust what an AI evaluation tells us. I got hooked when the coding agents we were
        benchmarking at imec turned out to be reading the answer key from git history, and then, once I took it away,
        confidently "remembering" fixes that didn't exist (107 of the 108 commit hashes they cited weren't real). Now
        I'm chasing the bigger version of that question in a SPAR project on evaluation awareness, and I plan to apply
        to PhD programs in fall 2027, for 2028 entry.
      </p>
      <p>
        By day I'm a product manager at aistack, imec's AI systems team in Belgium, where we run different AI workloads on real
        hardware, picking up various new research challenges as we progress into building a useful product for optimizing the AI stack. Thanks to my role and my own strengths, I'm also
        exploring technical AI governance next to empirical safety research: if regulation leans on evaluations as evidence, leaky or awareness-sensitive evals make that evidence shaky, and imec taught me how compute and chips actually get verified. Long term I'd like to continue enabling fantastic teams to thrive and have impact, but first my inner child wants a proper go at being a researcher and sticking with one problem.
      </p>
      <p>
        Before imec I did an MSc in HCI at Twente and Aalto, with a thesis on LLM evaluation at Iris.AI, ran releases as
        a product owner at Twipe, ran a grant program for open datasets in underrepresented European languages at
        Microsoft's AI for Good Lab, and worked on a multi-tenant LLM inference platform at OpenChip. I was also a
        statistics TA at Twente for two years, because yes, I like the math. Off the clock I hike long trails and play
        a gnome warlock.
      </p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="questions" aria-labelledby="questions">
    <h2 id="questions">The question I want to work on</h2>
    <ul class="plain">
      <li>
        How can we detect when a language model is scheming, cheating or reward hacking, and use those signals to
        steer it away from doing so?
        <span class="take">I think the traces are there, in the reasoning, the internals and the shortcuts models take,
        and better evals should turn them into something we can align against, not just report. The catch: train hard
        against a detector and you may just teach the model to hide from it, so I care as much about whether a signal
        survives being used as about finding it.</span>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="stance">
      Where I stand: big fan of open-weight models, AI for good (I worked in the lab, so duh) and AI for science.
      Anti-AI for exactly one thing, my personal blog posts. I write those myself.
    </p>
  </section>

  <div class="cards">
    <section class="card" aria-labelledby="research">
      <h2 id="research">Research</h2>
      <ul class="plain">
        {
          research.map((p) => (
            <li>
              <a href={href(p)}>{p.data.title}</a>
              <span class="meta">{p.data.venue ?? p.data.context}, {p.data.year}</span>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
      <p class="mono"><a href="/research/">All research →</a></p>
    </section>

    <section class="card" aria-labelledby="writing">
      <h2 id="writing">Writing</h2>
      <p class="label">At aistack, while working at imec</p>
      <ul class="plain">
        {
          writing.map((w) => (
            <li>
              <a href={w.data.url}>{w.data.title}</a>
              <span class="meta">{meta(w)}</span>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
      <p class="label sep">Personal blog</p>
      <ul class="plain">
        {
          personal.map((w) => (
            <li>
              <a href={w.data.url}>{w.data.title}</a>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
      <p class="mono"><a href="/writing/">All writing →</a></p>
    </section>
  </div>
  <script is:inline>
    (function () {
      var wrap = document.getElementById('portrait');
      var btn = document.getElementById('meme-toggle');
      var front = wrap.querySelector('.front');
      var back = wrap.querySelector('.back');
      btn.addEventListener('click', function () {
        var on = wrap.classList.toggle('flipped');
        btn.setAttribute('aria-pressed', String(on));
        btn.dataset.tip = on ? 'back to me' : 'reveal my fav meme template';
        front.setAttribute('aria-hidden', String(on));
        back.setAttribute('aria-hidden', String(!on));
      });
    })();
  </script>
</Base>
